NNAAMMEE
       start-stop-daemon - start and stop system daemon programs

SSYYNNOOPPSSIISS
       ssttaarrtt--ssttoopp--ddaaeemmoonn [_o_p_t_i_o_n...] _c_o_m_m_a_n_d

DDEESSCCRRIIPPTTIIOONN
       ssttaarrtt--ssttoopp--ddaaeemmoonn is used to control the creation and termination of
       system-level processes.  Using one of the matching options, ssttaarrtt--ssttoopp--
       ddaaeemmoonn can be configured to find existing instances of a running
       process.

       NNoottee:: Unless ----ppiidd or ----ppiiddffiillee are specified, ssttaarrtt--ssttoopp--ddaaeemmoonn
       behaves similar to kkiillllaallll(1).  ssttaarrtt--ssttoopp--ddaaeemmoonn will scan the process
       table looking for any processes which match the process name, parent
       pid, uid, and/or gid (if specified). Any matching process will prevent
       ----ssttaarrtt from starting the daemon. All matching processes will be sent
       the TERM signal (or the one specified via ----ssiiggnnaall or ----rreettrryy) if
       ----ssttoopp is specified. For daemons which have long-lived children which
       need to live through a ----ssttoopp, you must specify a pidfile.

